\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Mirror-twin facet locking and periodic vertical towers. (a)~Four-block alternating tower of square \#763 at the cubic 3D-print scale $\lambda =0.20$, alternating $\mathcal  G_\lambda (S)$ (green) with its mirror twin $\mathcal  G_\lambda (r^2\overline  S)=-\mathcal  G_\lambda (S)$ (blue); the golden contact joints are the centred knight's-move squares $\{13,14,15,16\}$ and $\{1,2,3,4\}$, which meet with $100\%$ vertex congruence and keep the column's centre of mass on the vertical $z$-axis (red dashed). (b)~Top-down $(x,y)$ footprint of square \#763 showing the dual knight's-move squares of area $A_{xy}=5$ and 2D footprint inradius $r=\sqrt  {5}/2\approx 1.12$ (while the 3D tilted-plane stability margin from $\mathbf  c_0$ is $d_{\mathrm  {stab}}=0.969$ at $\lambda =0.20$). (c)~Two-dimensional side-elevation joint and tipping schematic comparing the $2\theta _{\mathrm  {joint}}=22.6^\circ $ wedge mismatch of unrotated same-gem stacking against flush mirror-twin locking ($\mathbf  n_+^*=-\mathbf  n_+$) on a $\theta _{\mathrm  {joint}}=11.3^\circ $ base plinth. (d)~Census of critical tipping angles $\theta _{\mathrm  {tip}}$ over all $14{,}824$ facets at $\lambda =0.20$ and $\lambda =1.0$.}}{19}{figure.80}\protected@file@percent }
